1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,900
that rational expectations has been explicit,

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:05,920
but still you can go back centuries in economics

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:08,400
and still see that something like rational expectations

4
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,980
is in the minds of the economists.

5
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:13,800
So you just think about things like,

6
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:17,120
Adam Smith talking about the effect of a price control.

7
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:21,420
So if people had completely random views about the world,

8
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:23,720
or if people believed that up was down,

9
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:26,980
then price controls would not necessarily affect behavior.

10
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:29,160
So suppose, for example, that people just didn't believe

11
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:31,520
that price controls existed even when they did.

12
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:34,480
People are so oblivious, you have a minimum wage,

13
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:35,840
and you say, well, you realize that you have to go

14
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:37,040
and pay people's amount.

15
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:38,840
They're like, no, I don't.

16
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:39,820
No, I don't.

17
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:43,440
And therefore, I'm not gonna go and hire fewer people.

18
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:46,140
Right, if people really were that confused

19
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:47,760
about the way the world works,

20
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,440
then they would not be responding to incentives

21
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:52,160
in which they are unaware.

22
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:53,600
So whenever an economist says,

23
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,400
well, this is what the government does,

24
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:56,760
we'll change incentives in this way,

25
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:58,780
which leads to this behavior,

26
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:00,720
that last step requires something

27
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:02,560
like a rational expectation assumption

28
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:04,440
so that people's views about the world

29
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:06,880
are related to the way the world really is.

30
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:09,400
Pretty simple point.

31
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:12,040
All right, now what are the empirical implications

32
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,480
of rational expectations?

33
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:15,920
So the one that's really easy to test,

34
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:19,600
as we've said before, is no systematic errors.

35
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:32,600
All right, so no systematic errors.

36
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:36,880
In other words, the expectation of the error is zero.

37
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,320
There can be views that are too high,

38
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:40,300
views that are too low,

39
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:42,680
but on average, they balance out.

40
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:45,520
And then there's also the no correlation

41
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:46,960
with available information.

42
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,960
No correlation with available information.

43
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:56,520
That's one where it's a little bit harder to do

44
00:01:56,520 --> 00:01:58,000
because you can argue about

45
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,880
what information counts as available.

46
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:01,880
In the 1970s, a standard thing would be to say,

47
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:04,080
well, was it in yesterday's Wall Street Journal

48
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:05,080
that it's available?

49
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:07,800
All right, that kind of thing.

50
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:10,560
Today would be harder to do in a way,

51
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:13,280
so if you said, well, when is the price of Exxon available?

52
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:16,940
You might say, one second ago is when it's available.

53
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:19,120
And then you'd have to have your beliefs uncorrelated

54
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:20,960
with information as recently as that.

55
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:24,480
So anyway, big empirical literature

56
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:26,640
that's found deviations from rational expectations,

57
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:29,680
but I've also elementary probability theory,

58
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:32,560
and I'm gonna be playing devil's advocate.

59
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:36,080
But I will also give you some reservations along the way.

60
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:37,920
So let's see.

61
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:40,880
So since we're getting a little bit behind,

62
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:42,120
I'm gonna cut out section two,

63
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:43,840
so you don't need to know that.

64
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:45,000
Nothing else really builds on it,

65
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:46,800
just more of a fun topic.

66
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:49,800
So move on to section three.

67
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:52,240
So belief, perseverance, and confirmatory biases.

68
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:55,280
So remember, this Bayesian framework is about updating.

69
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:58,120
New information comes in, and what are you supposed to do?

70
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:03,160
Change your mind, change your mind.

71
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:04,920
And think about this.

72
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:06,800
Whenever someone says,

73
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:08,960
every day I become more convinced of X,

74
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:12,800
is that person likely to be a good Bayesian?

75
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:15,800
That would be a miracle.

76
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,000
It's like, wait a minute, that would,

77
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,360
because look, if you've got an information

78
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:22,520
that makes you think something is more and more likely,

79
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:24,360
then you should just update.

80
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:27,200
And then after you update, then from then on,

81
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:29,760
the information should no longer be more,

82
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:30,600
keep convincing them.

83
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:33,120
Instead, for example, if you,

84
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:35,680
every day I become more convinced that Brian's a jerk.

85
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:38,160
So once you have some information on that,

86
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:40,200
you say, well, it's really likely that he's a jerk.

87
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:42,520
And in that case, when the evidence comes in

88
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:43,360
that I'm a jerk, you say,

89
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:45,640
well, just about as jerky as I was expecting.

90
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:46,840
No surprise there.

91
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:48,480
So I don't think he's more of a jerk

92
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:50,280
just because he acted like a jerk again.

93
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,600
That's just what would be expected,

94
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:52,760
given my previous view.

95
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:56,280
You know, just like if every day Bill Gates

96
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:59,720
shows up wearing, or shows up driving a fancy new car,

97
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:01,480
he goes, every day I'm more and more convinced

98
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:02,880
he's a rich guy.

99
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:05,800
It's like, look, I'm already convinced.

100
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:09,000
He's driving the cars at exactly the level

101
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:10,240
you'd expect for a billionaire,

102
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:12,120
if his dad's really likes cars.

103
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:14,720
So really, you know, like you might act,

104
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:16,640
you might drive a fancy car and you say,

105
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:19,240
not quite as fancy as I was expecting.

106
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:21,760
And so I'm gonna think that he's less rich.

107
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:23,400
Right, whereas another person

108
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:25,080
could be driving the very same car

109
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:26,200
and you would say, hey,

110
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:27,640
a graduate student's driving that car,

111
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:29,840
that graduate student, that made me change my mind,

112
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:31,520
think that graduate student's really rich.

113
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:32,360
But if Bill Gates doing it,

114
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:34,080
it would make you think less of him

115
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:36,200
because you expect so much.

116
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:37,040
Yeah, Ash.

117
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:38,440
If this is saying every day,

118
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:40,320
I'm more and more convinced that Brian is a jerk,

119
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:42,240
is that equivalent to saying,

120
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,960
every day Brian surprises me more and more

121
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:45,880
with how much of a jerk he is?

122
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:47,400
Yes, yes, yes, exactly.

123
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:48,680
Every day there's more,

124
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:50,720
there's another surprise piece of evidence

125
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:52,160
in favor of jerkiness.

126
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:54,400
It's like, well, you've been surprised

127
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:55,520
like a hundred times in a row?

128
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:56,960
What's wrong with you?

129
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:58,000
Like, how could you?

130
00:04:58,000 --> 00:04:59,040
I mean, like that's,

131
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:00,880
makes as much sense as every day

132
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:03,000
someone stands behind a corner and says boo,

133
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,440
and every day you're like, ah, ah,

134
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:06,400
a hundred times in a row.

135
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:08,280
It's like, eventually it should get old.

136
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:10,080
Like, well, you always do that.

137
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:11,600
It's not surprising at all anymore.

138
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,840
Not scary at all anymore.

139
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:14,680
That's the idea.

140
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:16,920
All right, so anyway,

141
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:18,400
Bayesian framework's about updating

142
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:19,880
whenever someone says,

143
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:21,800
every day I become more convinced.

144
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:22,960
That is a strong sign

145
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:24,400
that you should just ignore what that person says

146
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:25,920
because they're a bad Bayesian.

147
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:27,600
Right?

148
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:29,120
Normally people say it as a brag.

149
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:31,560
It's like, I'm just so right all the time.

150
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:34,240
Everything keeps confirming what I thought.

151
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:35,760
It's like, well, essentially,

152
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:37,720
in other words, what you're saying is that

153
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:41,040
every day you fail to adequately adjust your belief

154
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:42,440
in response to information

155
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:44,160
so you can be surprised over and over again.

156
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:45,720
But let's go and listen to people

157
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:48,600
who every day things are either,

158
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:50,680
they're equally likely to move their mind

159
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:51,680
in either direction.

160
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:53,880
You know, generally, anytime someone says

161
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:57,520
I've never seen any evidence that was against this,

162
00:05:57,520 --> 00:05:59,240
it's like, never, ever.

163
00:06:00,480 --> 00:06:04,240
Funny, I'm seeing evidence against my views all the time.

164
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:06,840
Because given, like, once my view is confident,

165
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:09,280
any little error, it's like, hmm,

166
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:11,800
that's not what you'd expect if this theory is right.

167
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:13,440
Even if it's a small deviation.

168
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:14,280
Strange.

169
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:16,240
All right, so anyway,

170
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:18,520
Bayesian framework's all about updating.

171
00:06:18,520 --> 00:06:19,760
But there's a bunch of experiments showing

172
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:21,640
what's called belief perseverance,

173
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:23,400
where people stick with their initial view

174
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:25,680
even when contrary evidence pours in.

175
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:27,360
All right.

176
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,960
And there is a, you know,

177
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:32,360
there is a set of experiments finding that people

178
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:34,960
can actually be more accurate with less information.

179
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:37,920
All right, now this is kind of a weird experiment,

180
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:39,960
but it's still pretty famous

181
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:40,880
and people are talking about it,

182
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:42,320
so I'll share it with you.

183
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:44,920
So this is an experiment where

184
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:47,880
you either show someone a blurry image

185
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:50,080
and then you gradually make it clearer,

186
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:54,640
or you just show them the last frame of the series.

187
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:57,400
So in other words, you know, you only get,

188
00:06:57,400 --> 00:06:58,640
so in both cases,

189
00:06:58,640 --> 00:07:01,040
so imagine that you see 30 pictures,

190
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:03,400
you know, 30 pictures of the picture,

191
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:06,400
30 screenshots of the picture

192
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:08,360
getting more, less and less blurry,

193
00:07:08,360 --> 00:07:09,880
although the last picture's still kind of blurry,

194
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:11,040
that's key.

195
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:13,040
Or you would just see picture number 30.

196
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:15,400
And in these experiments,

197
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:18,160
it seems like people only see picture number 30,

198
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:20,680
are better able to guess what the picture really shows

199
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:23,120
than people who saw a whole series of pictures

200
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,840
of the thing gradually coming into focus.

201
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:27,640
And the usual story is,

202
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:29,600
the people who saw 30 pictures

203
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:32,920
formed a theory about what they were seeing early on,

204
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:34,480
and then once they formed the theory,

205
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:35,400
then every picture's like,

206
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:37,760
oh yeah, that's the nose of the penguin.

207
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:39,520
Oh yeah, yeah, yeah, that's his wing.

208
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:42,400
And each one, they are imposing their theory

209
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:43,680
upon the evidence.

210
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:45,320
Whereas if you only see the last one,

211
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:47,800
then you don't have a chance

212
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:49,680
to go and fit everything into this

213
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:51,000
emerging body of information,

214
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,040
then you just look at the last

215
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:55,080
and of course most informative picture

216
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:56,560
and form your belief on that.

217
00:07:57,480 --> 00:07:59,680
So that is one experiment.

218
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:01,560
Now, important to remember that in the experiment,

219
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:03,200
they never show you a clear picture.

220
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:05,360
It goes from being super blurry

221
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:07,200
to kind of blurry.

222
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:08,800
If it eventually was crystal clear,

223
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:11,240
then this result almost certainly wouldn't work.

224
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:12,080
Right?

225
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:14,040
If in the end, it's like the Joker for Batman

226
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:16,720
and you thought it was a bird,

227
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:19,440
the actual penguin, the bird, not the super villain,

228
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:22,200
then you'll, I think, be very unlikely

229
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:23,880
that someone would make that mistake.

230
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:25,800
But if you keep them,

231
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:27,360
if they're blurry before and afterwards,

232
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:28,760
but you give people a chance

233
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:30,880
to start getting their mental gears turning

234
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:33,680
and thinking, oh yeah, that's kind of what it is,

235
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:35,680
that's where the problem emerges.

236
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:37,280
Yes, is there a question there?

237
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:40,880
All right.

238
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:43,560
Okay, now other experiments find a stronger effect.

239
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:44,880
Once you believe in hypothesis,

240
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:47,000
then they get increasingly confident.

241
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:48,280
Right, per the saying.

242
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:50,440
Every day, the evidence is mounting.

243
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:52,480
Like more and more convinced every day.

244
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:54,840
It's like, wow.

245
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:57,680
So how agnostic were you at the beginning

246
00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:59,360
if you've spent the last 30 years

247
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:03,160
becoming more and more convinced every single day?

248
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:06,720
Wow, it's like, seems like you'd be

249
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:09,200
quite agnostic initially.

250
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:10,200
All right.

251
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:11,400
So anyway, the story here is that

252
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:13,400
more likely to notice confirming evidence

253
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:15,240
and to misinterpret ambiguous evidence

254
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:16,440
as additional support.

255
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:17,480
And this has a special name.

256
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:20,040
It's called confirmatory bias or confirmation bias.

257
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:29,000
All right, so, simple example.

258
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,840
So yeah, going back to the Brian is a jerk story.

259
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:33,320
Once you believe this story,

260
00:09:33,320 --> 00:09:36,280
then you might just sort of follow me around

261
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:38,800
and look for examples of me being a jerk.

262
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:41,360
And then you say, look, the evidence is overwhelming.

263
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:44,080
I've got 30 different instances.

264
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:47,080
Right, but that's not a very good way to test the theory.

265
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:49,120
Right, it's one thing you should be doing,

266
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:50,480
but what should you also be doing

267
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:51,880
in order to test the theory?

268
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,480
Yes, making a list of all the times I'm not a jerk

269
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:58,720
and looking for disconfirmatory evidence.

270
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:00,080
And then once you've got all that,

271
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:02,040
you say, well, what would the ratio be

272
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:03,640
for a jerk versus an un-jerk?

273
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:05,520
Right, and then it might be,

274
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:08,520
oh, actually, this is totally expected.

275
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:10,800
Right, you know, just like someone might,

276
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:13,440
so a communist might look at the American economy

277
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:14,720
and then go and make a list

278
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:17,000
of a thousand horrible things about it

279
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,600
and then say, communism is true, obviously.

280
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:21,400
Who can deny the facts?

281
00:10:21,400 --> 00:10:24,640
It's like, well, all you did was go to a world

282
00:10:24,640 --> 00:10:26,920
or a country of 300 million people

283
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:28,920
and go and find a thousand problems,

284
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:30,760
which can't be that hard, actually,

285
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:32,520
and then say, you're right,

286
00:10:32,520 --> 00:10:34,960
that's a terrible way of approaching it.

287
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:36,040
You'd want it, like, again,

288
00:10:36,040 --> 00:10:38,840
you could go and list all the bad things

289
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:40,800
and then list all the good things.

290
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:42,200
You could also do random sampling.

291
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:43,600
That's another nice approach.

292
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:45,800
Just say, look, I'm gonna go and take a look every minute,

293
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:47,600
and I'm just gonna go and look at a random news article

294
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:49,920
and see what's going on.

295
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:52,520
Why didn't that would be a much better way of doing it, too?

296
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:54,840
Right, there's many better ways of doing it

297
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:56,480
than just going and starting with a theory

298
00:10:56,480 --> 00:10:58,560
and then looking around for every story

299
00:10:58,560 --> 00:10:59,880
that advances it.

300
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:04,840
This is the reasonable core of Popperian falsificationism,

301
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:07,080
where he says, yes, give it,

302
00:11:07,080 --> 00:11:08,680
but I'm not sure whether he said

303
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:10,440
whether he actually framed it in terms of psychology.

304
00:11:10,440 --> 00:11:12,840
At least his followers have never done so to me,

305
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:15,760
but the idea is if there's a theory you think is right,

306
00:11:15,760 --> 00:11:18,600
look around for all the reasons that it's wrong.

307
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:21,600
You'll be very energetic in trying to find reasons

308
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:22,800
to change your mind about something.

309
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:25,520
We'll be looking for exceptions, right?

310
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:28,800
Like if you think that the economy is verbally competitive,

311
00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:31,680
you might just spend a day walking around looking at verbs

312
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:33,960
and try to find things that look like they don't fit

313
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:36,240
with a verbally competitive model.

314
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:39,560
Like go to a movie theater at the matinee,

315
00:11:39,560 --> 00:11:42,560
and you will see an overwhelming contradiction

316
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:45,000
to the verbally competitive model.

317
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Whoa, 90% of the seats are empty, but the price was $8,

318
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:55,120
and the marginal cost appears to be maybe eight cents tops.

319
00:11:55,120 --> 00:11:57,560
So the verbally competitive model says the price will,

320
00:11:57,560 --> 00:11:59,200
the price, the marginal cost is eight cents.

321
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:01,160
Verbally competitive model says the price will be?

322
00:12:01,160 --> 00:12:02,280
Eight cents.

323
00:12:02,280 --> 00:12:03,320
Eight cents.

324
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:05,800
It's $8, how can that be?

325
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:08,360
Right, so these are the kinds of things to be looking for,

326
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:10,200
or if you're looking for,

327
00:12:10,200 --> 00:12:13,560
so you know, for price differences, try to explain them.

328
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:15,520
Right, so verbally competitive model says

329
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:16,880
the different products can have different prices,

330
00:12:16,880 --> 00:12:19,280
but only if the costs are different.

331
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:22,960
Right, and then you might even go and look at things

332
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:24,520
that initially seem to support the model and say,

333
00:12:24,520 --> 00:12:26,880
wait a second, actually they don't really support the model.

334
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:29,400
Like think about this, every flavor of ice cream

335
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:32,160
is the same price within a brand normally.

336
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:33,880
What are the odds that the cost of producing

337
00:12:33,880 --> 00:12:35,120
every one of those flavors of ice cream

338
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:37,360
is exactly equal to the penny?

339
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:41,120
Seems hard to believe.

340
00:12:41,120 --> 00:12:41,960
Yeah, it seems to me.

341
00:12:41,960 --> 00:12:43,920
So again, like the only theory that I know that makes sense

342
00:12:43,920 --> 00:12:46,040
is that they vary the content,

343
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:47,640
the quantity of the expensive ingredients

344
00:12:47,640 --> 00:12:50,840
to hit a certain price, but again, that's,

345
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:52,520
so like they're telling me that the cost of mint

346
00:12:52,520 --> 00:12:55,760
is exactly equal to the cost of chocolate syrup.

347
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:57,720
Like, that's weird.

348
00:12:57,720 --> 00:13:00,000
Like what a world that these things would all be,

349
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,560
so it would all be equal in price.

350
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:04,920
So there's many opportunities to go and look

351
00:13:04,920 --> 00:13:06,880
for these counter examples.

352
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:09,880
So it's looking for the model to fail in interesting ways.

353
00:13:09,880 --> 00:13:11,640
Yeah, yeah, there's lots and lots and lots

354
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:12,480
of interesting ways to fail.

355
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:14,960
And then of course, the fact that you've found

356
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:16,240
what appears to be a counter example,

357
00:13:16,240 --> 00:13:18,880
it might be like on third, on second thought,

358
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:20,120
you say counter example, third thought,

359
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:21,560
maybe it's not really a good counter example

360
00:13:21,560 --> 00:13:22,840
to think about it more.

361
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:24,480
Anyway, these are standard tactics

362
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:27,800
for avoiding this confirmation bias.

363
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:29,520
All right, now in terms of experiments

364
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:32,320
on confirmation bias, my very favorite one in the world

365
00:13:32,320 --> 00:13:36,520
involves a real issue where if we were doing this room,

366
00:13:36,520 --> 00:13:38,120
I would first of all go and find out, right,

367
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:39,840
so who supports the death penalty, who opposes it?

368
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:42,520
Great, all right, then I would go and randomly assign

369
00:13:42,520 --> 00:13:45,760
equal numbers of each group to two different rooms.

370
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:49,480
In room one, I give you two kinds of econometric studies.

371
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:52,640
One has panel data that shows the death penalty to hers.

372
00:13:52,640 --> 00:13:54,360
Another has cross-sectional data

373
00:13:54,360 --> 00:13:56,440
showing that it doesn't deter.

374
00:13:56,440 --> 00:13:59,000
The other room has panel data showing

375
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:00,520
that it's penalty does not deter

376
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:03,320
and cross-sectional evidence showing that it does.

377
00:14:03,320 --> 00:14:05,480
What happens when you do this experiment?

378
00:14:05,480 --> 00:14:08,820
Everyone gets more confident in their view, everyone.

379
00:14:08,820 --> 00:14:11,160
All four groups, right?

380
00:14:11,160 --> 00:14:13,260
And how does this happen?

381
00:14:13,260 --> 00:14:16,840
Well, in the room where the panel data is pro-death penalty,

382
00:14:16,840 --> 00:14:19,520
what do the pro-death penalty people say?

383
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:20,340
Of course.

384
00:14:20,340 --> 00:14:23,160
Yeah, panel data is the rival data, cross-sectional,

385
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:25,520
ugh, all right, and of course,

386
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:28,080
the people that don't like the death penalty in that room

387
00:14:28,080 --> 00:14:30,200
reverse their econometric judgment.

388
00:14:30,200 --> 00:14:32,800
Cross-sectional is so much more probative than panel data.

389
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:34,240
You can do anything with panel data.

390
00:14:34,240 --> 00:14:35,920
Meanwhile, over in the other room,

391
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:39,680
your counterpart who has the same prejudice that you do

392
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:42,080
but received reversed information

393
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:44,200
is rationalizing the very same view

394
00:14:44,200 --> 00:14:46,600
based upon the methodological superiority

395
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:48,100
of the opposite kind of study.

396
00:14:49,280 --> 00:14:51,000
So, pretty striking example.

397
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,960
Okay, so questions about that?

398
00:14:55,960 --> 00:14:56,960
So, that one's fine.

399
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:02,280
All right, and this is something where they go like,

400
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:05,200
more and more evidence like this has come out.

401
00:15:05,200 --> 00:15:07,000
It's fine to say that more and more evidence has come out.

402
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:10,420
Not fine to say that I keep getting more convinced, right?

403
00:15:10,420 --> 00:15:12,320
Because to keep getting more convinced,

404
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:14,960
the evidence would have to come out to a degree of,

405
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:17,280
it would keep coming out to a greater degree than expected

406
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:19,520
given how much evidence we've already got, right?

407
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:21,280
Because if there's a whole bunch of great studies

408
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:22,400
showing something,

409
00:15:22,400 --> 00:15:24,720
what should you expect to happen in the future?

410
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:28,920
More great studies that keep saying the same thing, right?

411
00:15:28,920 --> 00:15:30,440
So, when those studies come out,

412
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:32,480
that's where you say, yeah, pretty much what we had expected

413
00:15:32,480 --> 00:15:34,820
given how solidly established this was.

414
00:15:34,820 --> 00:15:38,900
And you can even have studies that are quite supportive

415
00:15:38,900 --> 00:15:41,400
but not quite as supportive as you thought they would be

416
00:15:41,400 --> 00:15:44,680
leading you to lose some of your confidence.

417
00:15:44,680 --> 00:15:47,000
And again, if you've ever said things like this,

418
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,120
saying, look, I thought, well, the evidence is strong,

419
00:15:49,120 --> 00:15:51,600
it's not as strong as I expected it to be at this point,

420
00:15:51,600 --> 00:15:54,480
and so I've reduced my confidence in the theory,

421
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:57,380
which is a very weird thing for a human being to say.

422
00:15:57,380 --> 00:15:58,360
But if you've heard someone like,

423
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:00,200
if that kind of thinking is occurring to you,

424
00:16:00,200 --> 00:16:04,600
you are really internalizing Bayesianism as a way of life

425
00:16:04,600 --> 00:16:06,720
rather than just an equation,

426
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:09,680
because what goes to the equation if people don't live it?

427
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:13,680
All right.

428
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:15,400
All right, now, more general terms.

429
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:18,360
There is some evidence of systematic overconfidence.

430
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:21,280
How do you test for overconfidence?

431
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:24,600
Anyone here read the blogger Scott Alexander?

432
00:16:24,600 --> 00:16:27,880
Yeah, so, every year Scott makes a bunch of predictions

433
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:30,120
with probabilities, and then at the end of the year,

434
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:34,160
he goes and he graphs the subject of probabilities

435
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:35,580
he had at the beginning of the year

436
00:16:35,580 --> 00:16:38,640
with the object of probabilities of what really happens.

437
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:40,720
Right, and how does that work?

438
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:44,440
Well, so, when people do this,

439
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:46,760
they normally code everything

440
00:16:46,760 --> 00:16:49,800
so that everything is at least a 50% probability.

441
00:16:49,800 --> 00:16:52,960
Otherwise, you should just believe the opposite thing.

442
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:54,920
So anyway, so the normal thing here

443
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:58,320
is you've got a graph, and it starts at 0.5,

444
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:02,040
goes to one, and over here, 0.5, also goes to one,

445
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:04,080
and here are your subject of probabilities

446
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:09,480
before you actually know what happened.

447
00:17:09,480 --> 00:17:11,480
And here are the object of probabilities

448
00:17:12,440 --> 00:17:13,960
of what actually occurred,

449
00:17:13,960 --> 00:17:15,720
the frequencies with which they occurred.

450
00:17:15,720 --> 00:17:20,560
Now, if you have neither over nor underconfidence,

451
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:22,360
this will be a nice 45-degree line,

452
00:17:23,680 --> 00:17:26,560
where half the stuff that you assign

453
00:17:26,560 --> 00:17:29,320
a 50% probability to actually occurs,

454
00:17:29,320 --> 00:17:32,240
where 60% of the stuff that you assign

455
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:34,640
a 60% probability to occurs,

456
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:36,000
70% of the stuff that you assign

457
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,240
a 70% probability to actually occurs,

458
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:39,760
100% of the stuff that you assign

459
00:17:39,760 --> 00:17:41,880
a 100% probability to occurs.

460
00:17:41,880 --> 00:17:45,480
That is what's called perfect calibration,

461
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:48,240
where you are neither over nor underconfidence.

462
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:52,560
On the other hand, if your actual line,

463
00:17:52,560 --> 00:17:54,480
this 45-degree line right up there,

464
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:57,800
if your actual confidence looks like that,

465
00:17:57,800 --> 00:18:00,960
remember, you can't have a probability more than one.

466
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:03,640
I guess if you could fill in something 110%, sure,

467
00:18:03,640 --> 00:18:05,720
but that doesn't make any sense.

468
00:18:05,720 --> 00:18:08,440
Anyway, so if your line looks like that,

469
00:18:08,440 --> 00:18:10,760
are you over or underconfident?

470
00:18:14,280 --> 00:18:17,000
Yeah, so this is where you say that,

471
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,680
so you have a 60% probability that occurs,

472
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:22,520
and this actually goes and occurs even more often.

473
00:18:23,400 --> 00:18:25,800
Yes, so that's since you can say you're underconfident,

474
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:28,080
but then you can have this one here, this one here,

475
00:18:28,080 --> 00:18:31,440
that's what we generally see in the real world.

476
00:18:31,440 --> 00:18:33,880
So very typical that if someone says

477
00:18:33,880 --> 00:18:37,360
they're 100% confident, they're right 80% of the time.

478
00:18:37,360 --> 00:18:38,640
100 equals 80.

479
00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:42,440
90 equals 70, so on, so on.

480
00:18:42,440 --> 00:18:44,880
I think for like 50%, I think people actually,

481
00:18:44,880 --> 00:18:47,400
if you say, I can't actually remember

482
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:50,280
whether even odds, whether you actually are,

483
00:18:50,280 --> 00:18:51,120
whether that means you're actually

484
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:53,160
right 40% of the time or not, but anyway.

485
00:18:53,160 --> 00:18:55,640
So a lot of evidence of this overconfidence.

486
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:01,480
All right, a lot of evidence of this overconfidence.

487
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:06,480
Okay, now, this evidence, however, is not totally solid.

488
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:08,000
Again, that German psychologist,

489
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:09,480
Gerhard Gigerenzer, points out,

490
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:10,600
and this has been acknowledged

491
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:12,040
by people who've done the regular evidence,

492
00:19:12,040 --> 00:19:14,040
although per confirmation bias,

493
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:16,680
what do they say about Gigerenzer's evidence?

494
00:19:16,680 --> 00:19:17,520
It's not good enough.

495
00:19:17,520 --> 00:19:19,240
Yeah, yeah, no, no, no, wrong way of doing it,

496
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:20,320
it doesn't count.

497
00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:22,600
All right, but anyway, so Gigerenzer says

498
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:26,040
this works if you ask people question by question.

499
00:19:26,040 --> 00:19:29,000
So for example, if you give people an SAT test,

500
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:30,680
and then for each question you say,

501
00:19:30,680 --> 00:19:31,880
probability you got that one right,

502
00:19:31,880 --> 00:19:32,960
probably you got that one right,

503
00:19:32,960 --> 00:19:35,760
probably you got the other one right, then this works.

504
00:19:35,760 --> 00:19:38,720
But on the other hand, if you ask people the question,

505
00:19:38,720 --> 00:19:40,760
what fraction do you think you got right?

506
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:43,520
Gigerenzer says, there actually,

507
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:45,760
there's not much evidence of overconfidence.

508
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:47,520
So there's something about thinking about it

509
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:49,680
question by question that where it looks like

510
00:19:49,680 --> 00:19:51,760
your odd people have high overconfidence,

511
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:54,080
whereas if you ask them in general terms,

512
00:19:54,080 --> 00:19:56,760
like what fraction of other answers will be correct,

513
00:19:56,760 --> 00:19:58,880
then at least there's greatly reduced

514
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:00,680
evidence of overconfidence.


